\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Docker Compose topology for the two Virtual Knowledge Graphs. Each Ontop container waits for its database's healthcheck before starting, and both mount the same JDBC driver directory (dashed arrows) --- a workaround for a missing-driver defect in the base Ontop image.}}{22}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces The Movie ontology has no native SHACL shapes, so a one-time script generates them from its existing \texttt {rdfs:domain}/\texttt {rdfs:range} declarations before the schema parser can consume it. This m-schema JSON is the same artifact Section~\ref {sec:rec-validation}'s schema-grounding check embeds and thresholds against.}}{25}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Both the real HTTP endpoint and the internal development harness converge on \texttt {handle\_recommend()}, which validates the request, persists it, and returns a response. This is the extension point the recommender core (Chapter~\ref {ch:recommender}) is built inside, without any further change to the contract, the log store, or the harness.}}{27}{figure.caption.10}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces The behavioral-profiling pipeline. The catalog vocabulary (top) tags search queries and implicitly defines the category space watch events are grouped into. The actual (left) and stated (right) branches each produce a per-user/month/category intensity table, feeding both the per-user JSON profiles and a divergence score; the temporal join (bottom left) produces a separate, row-level output.}}{31}{figure.caption.11}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces The four-stage recommender core. The behavioral profile (right) feeds both the generation prompt and the ranking score directly --- the two concrete points where the thesis's stated-vs-actual signal shapes the final output, not just the initial candidate pool.}}{34}{figure.caption.13}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces The evaluation's ablation flow. Both conditions run the identical, real recommender-core functions; the only difference is whether the behavioral profile is passed. Scoring is decoupled from what each condition was allowed to see --- both are graded against the same real held-out ground truth.}}{43}{figure.caption.15}%
